## 1. Adversarial constructions (all executed, not just traced)

- "H & 123" -> "H", "H 123" -> "H" — IDENTICAL. Nit #1 dead: fusion's
  new right-neighbor letter check (`!hasNoLetters(fields[i+1])`) blocks
  fusion when the right neighbor is letterless, so "&" stays standalone
  and is dropped by the existing letterless rule, same as if it had never
  been there.
- "A & & B" -> "A B", "A && B" -> "A B" — IDENTICAL. Nit #2 dead: at the
  first "&" (i=1) the right-neighbor check reads fields[2]="&", which is
  letterless, so fusion is blocked; the second "&" (i=2) then fails the
  new LEFT-neighbor check (`!hasNoLetters(out[len(out)-1])`) since
  out[-1] is the unfused "&" itself. Both "&" tokens stay standalone and
  are dropped. "A && B" tokenizes as a single "&&" token (strings.Fields
  never splits on internal chars), which was already letterless-dropped
  pre- and post-P11. Both now "A B".
- "& & &" -> "". First "&" fails the `len(out)>0` guard (nothing to fuse
  left of it), second and third each fail the new left-neighbor letter
  check (left neighbor is itself an unfused "&"). All three tokens
  letterless-dropped. Self-consistent (nothing to be consistent WITH —
  no letters anywhere), idempotent, no panic.
- "H & 123 & M" -> "H M"; "H 123 M" -> "H M" — IDENTICAL, and this is
  the deeper form of nit #1 the task flagged (a chain, not just a pair).
  Traced AND executed under the OLD (P10) fusion rule for comparison:
  pre-P11, "H & 123 & M" fused straight through to the single degenerate
  token "H&123&M" (no letter gate), which does NOT match "H 123 M" ->
  "H M" (a 2-token key) — that was the live inconsistency. Post-P11: at
  i=1 the "&" fails the right-neighbor check (fields[2]="123" is
  letterless) so it's left standalone; "123" becomes its own token; at
  i=3 the "&" fails the LEFT-neighbor check (out[-1] is now "123",
  letterless) so it's also left standalone. Both "&"s and "123" are
  letterless-dropped, leaving "H M" — matching "H 123 M" exactly. The
  letterless middle token correctly blocks fusion from bridging across
  it in both directions.
- "12 & 34" -> "". Both tokens letterless, "&"'s left-neighbor check
  fails immediately (out[-1]="12" is letterless), all three dropped.
  Confirmed identical to pre-P11 result (pre-P11 this fused to "12&34",
  itself letterless, also dropped to "") — no behavior change on
  both-letterless inputs, consistent with the P10 verdict's finding that
  this case was already safe.
- "Á & B" -> "Á&B". Go's `unicode.IsLetter` recognizes combining/accented
  Latin letters (category L*), so hasNoLetters("Á") is false and fusion
  proceeds normally — confirms the letter gate isn't ASCII-only and
  doesn't introduce a Unicode-specific hole.
- Pinned-behavior spot checks executed alongside: "H & M" -> "H&M" (the
  original P10 two-letter-brand fix, unaffected since both neighbors are
  lettered), "AT&T STORE 44 & 45" -> "AT&T STORE", "STORE 44 & 45" ->
  "STORE" (both-letterless case from the P10 verdict, unchanged).

## 2. No new merge path; P10 behavior pinned

The functional diff is exactly one `if` condition gaining two additional
`&&`-ed conjuncts (`!hasNoLetters(out[len(out)-1])`,
`!hasNoLetters(fields[i+1])`) on the SAME branch that performs fusion.
Boolean narrowing: new-condition ⇒ old-condition always holds (A&&B&&C
implies A), so fuseAmpersands fires on a subset of the inputs it fired on
before — it can only leave MORE "&" tokens standalone (which the
pre-existing, untouched letterless-drop step then removes), never fuse a
pair it didn't already fuse. Fusion is the only token-merging mechanism
in this package; GroupKeys/shouldMerge/tokenSet/isSubset (the actual
merge-decision code) have zero diff lines. Since fusion strictly
shrinks, the set of possible fused-token outputs strictly shrinks too,
so no NEW subset-mergeable key shape can appear that didn't already
exist under P10 — narrowing a token-*producing* step cannot manufacture
a new *merge* opportunity in the downstream token-subset rule. The test
diff (merchants_test.go) is purely additive per `git diff` — grepped for
`-` lines matching existing test entries and found none; all prior
Normalize/GroupKeys/GroupTransactions cases, including the P10
brand-fusion pins ("H & M" -> "H&M" etc.), are unchanged text and pass
under this run.

## 3. Idempotence

Normalize(Normalize(x)) == Normalize(x) asserted programmatically for
every construction above (all pass fully-fused or fully-dropped states
that are stable under re-normalization — a fused token like "H&123&M"
never arises post-P11 for these inputs, and even where it would, e.g.
"H & M" -> "H&M", re-running Normalize on "H&M" hits no "&" as a
standalone token so it passes through unchanged). Zero violations.

